**Read:** [Physical Geology, second edition — Steven Earle](../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX), chapter 3 in full; [Introduction to Historical Geology — Estrada and contributing authors](../sources/Introduction-to-Historical-Geology.lingua#r_1JKSSECEDW0M9QG30TJ1D9KHZD), the complete igneous-rock and magma/history material; [Introduction to Geology — Perron and Jagoutz, MIT 12.001](../sources/MIT-Introduction-to-Geology.lingua#r_423FFVY46CGEN2F2263F95KX6N), the igneous/mantle notes and exercises.

Igneous interpretations connect source material, pressure/temperature/composition, partial melting, melt transport, crystallization, and emplacement. A magma can contain liquid, crystals, and volatile components. Distinguish source composition from the composition of a partial melt and the final rock; material transfer and reaction can change each.

Cooling and crystallization are governed by phase relations and kinetics under conditions. Textures, mineral relationships, inclusions, cross-cutting contacts, chilled margins, and other field evidence can constrain a history. A simplified crystallization sequence is a useful model whose assumptions and scope need explanation.

Fractional crystallization, mixing, assimilation, and later alteration can produce overlapping compositional patterns. A single elemental plot or rock name does not uniquely select a process. Read how field context, mineral chemistry, isotopic evidence, and quantitative balances can distinguish candidate accounts.

**Work:** interpret an identified textbook intrusive-body example from observations to competing histories. Draw the material/energy and relative-time account, then explain a simple hypothetical partial-melting or crystal-separation balance. Identify what data would test its assumptions rather than claiming the calculation establishes the real history.

Read the entire intrusive-igneous chapter and relevant historical/lecture material. Volcanic processes follow in the tectonic sequence; the more detailed mineralogy/petrology and geochemistry studies will deepen phase equilibria, analysis, and research without treating these basic relationships as unrelated new knowledge.

